      {
        "id": "def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Two-body phase-space and formal coefficient data",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and use the energy-vector, metric, and unit conventions of [[def-qft-example-data]]. A rest-energy parameter $\\mathsf M\\geq0$ is measured in joules. Its positive shell is parameterized by $p=(E,\\mathbf p)$ with $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+\\mathsf M^2}>0$; the shell measure is $d\\nu_{\\mathsf M}=d^3\\mathbf p/((2\\pi)^3\\,2E)$. The value $\\mathsf M=0$, $\\mathbf p=0$ is excluded.\n\nLet $\\mathsf M_1,\\mathsf M_2\\geq0$ and let $s>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$. Define\n$$k_*=\\frac{\\sqrt{[s-(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2][s-(\\mathsf M_1-\\mathsf M_2)^2]}}{2\\sqrt{s}}, \\qquad E_i^*=\\sqrt{k_*^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}.$$\nBoth factors under the square root are positive, so $k_*>0$. On the Euclidean unit sphere $S^2$, let $d\\Omega$ be the chart surface measure of [[lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure]]. The center-of-momentum two-body phase-space measure is the finite Borel measure\n$$d\\Phi_2=\\frac{k_*}{16\\pi^2\\sqrt{s}}\\,d\\Omega, \\qquad \\mathbf n\\longmapsto ((E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n)).$$\n\nFor future energy four-vectors $p_1,p_2$ with nonnegative rest energies $\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}},\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, define the invariant flux when its radicand is nonnegative by\n$$F(p_1,p_2)=4\\sqrt{(p_1\\cdot p_2)^2-\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}}^2\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}^2}, \\qquad p\\cdot q=h(p,q).$$\nLet $\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ and $\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}$ be finite nonempty initial and final label sets, respectively, and let $w_i\\geq0$ for $i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ satisfy $\\sum_iw_i=1$. A spin-resolved formal amplitude is a family of dimensionless Borel functions $\\mathcal A_{if}:S^2\\to\\mathbb C$, indexed by $i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}$ and $f\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}$. Define the Borel function\n$$\\overline{|\\mathcal A(\\mathbf n)|^2}:=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm in}}w_i\\sum_{f\\in\\mathcal S_{\\rm out}}|\\mathcal A_{if}(\\mathbf n)|^2.$$\nFor a final pair, set $S_f=2$ when the two final particles are identical and $S_f=1$ when they are distinguishable. When the incoming flux is positive, $F>0$, define the center-of-momentum differential cross-section coefficient measure by\n$$d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{F S_f}\\, \\overline{|\\mathcal A(\\mathbf n)|^2}\\,d\\Phi_2.$$\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>0$ and a specified single-channel Borel formal decay amplitude $\\mathcal G:S^2\\to\\mathbb C$ measured in joules, define the extended nonnegative coefficient\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{1}{2\\hbar\\mathsf M_0 S_f} \\int_{S^2}|\\mathcal G(\\mathbf n)|^2\\,d\\Phi_2.$$\nIt is a finite rate coefficient when this integral is finite; otherwise the displayed integral has value $+\\infty$.\nThese are mathematical coefficient maps for the explicitly specified formal amplitudes; their definitions assert no exact interacting scattering operator or decay law.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-example-data",
          "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
