      {
        "id": "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Clifford traces and normalized spin completeness",
        "statement": "Use the finite Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], and the separate geometric metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and algebraic contraction $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Write $p=(E,\\mathbf p)$ for an energy four-vector with components in joules, $M\\geq0$ for a rest-energy parameter in joules, and $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+M^2}>0$. In the natural-length variables of the Dirac definition, $\\mathbf k=\\mathbf p/(\\hbar c)$, $\\mu=M/(\\hbar c)$, and $\\omega=E/(\\hbar c)$.",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-example-data",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 4, sections 4.3 and 4.7",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S4.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter; Clifford algebra, Pauli matrices, positive- and negative-frequency Dirac solutions, and finite spinor examples."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-posted prepublication draft, sections 46–47",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted textbook draft; printed pp. 292–297, spin sums and gamma-matrix trace identities."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Complete two-body phase space and coefficient flux map",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], and use [[def-qft-example-data]] and [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. A proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map is a real linear $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and $\\Lambda$ preserving the future cone $E>0$. For future energy four-vectors in the algebraic metric $h=(+,-,-,-)$, the shell measure $d^3\\mathbf p/((2\\pi)^3\\,2E)$ is invariant under every such map. Write $E_i(r)=\\sqrt{r^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}$. Let $f$ be a nonnegative Borel function on the product of the positive shells of [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. The standard two-body on-shell integral\n$$(2\\pi)^4\\int d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_1}(k_1)d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_2}(k_2)\\, \\delta^{(4)}(P-k_1-k_2)\\,f(k_1,k_2)$$\nis interpreted by first evaluating the spatial delta in $k_2$, then the one-dimensional energy delta by the increasing radial change of variable $r\\mapsto E_1(r)+E_2(r)$. Explicitly, after the spatial evaluation its radial action is $\\int_0^\\infty g(r)\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r))\\,dr=g(k_*)/(d(E_1+E_2)/dr|_{k_*})$ for nonnegative Borel $g$; this is the pushforward evaluation under that $C^1$ diffeomorphism, not a product of distributions. It equals $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_2$ from the Definition. The threshold root is $k_*$ in that Definition, and\n$$\\int d\\Phi_2=\\frac{k_*}{4\\pi\\sqrt{s}}.$$\nFor equal rest energies $\\mathsf M_1=\\mathsf M_2=\\mathsf M$, set $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$; then $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\int d\\Phi_2=\\beta/(8\\pi)$. For future incoming energy four-vectors $p_1,p_2$ with $p_1+p_2=P$ and nonnegative rest energies $\\mathsf M_{1,\\mathrm{in}},\\mathsf M_{2,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, let $p_*$ be their center-of-momentum spatial momentum magnitude. Their invariant flux is $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. For equal incoming rest energies $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ with $s\\geq4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$, define $\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2/s}$; then $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. For finite nonempty incoming and outgoing label sets, normalized initial weights, and the Borel amplitude family of the Definition, whenever $F>0$ the cross-section coefficient map gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\, \\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\, \\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$ decaying to two equal rest-energy daughters with constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$ when the daughters are identical. This is a coefficient calculation, not a claim of exponential survival.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-example-data",
          "def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data",
          "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness",
          "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 3, sections 3.6.2–3.6.3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter; invariant decay phase-space, center-of-momentum flux, and differential cross-section normalization."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-posted prepublication draft, section 11",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted textbook draft; printed pp. 93–99, two-body kinematics, shell measure, flux, phase-space integral, and symmetry factors."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-example-massive-tree-annihilation",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Full-mass unpolarized formal pair-production coefficient",
        "statement": "Let $m,M\\geq0$ be the electron and muon rest energies in joules. Let $p_1,p_2$ be future electron/positron energy four-vectors of rest energy $m$, and $k_1,k_2$ future muon/antimuon energy four-vectors of rest energy $M$, with $p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$, $s=P^2>4\\max(m^2,M^2)$, and the algebraic metric $h=(+,-,-,-)$. Use the dimensionless coupling $g=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}$ from [[def-qft-example-data]]. Adopt only the formal single-photon tree coefficient",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-example-data",
          "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness",
          "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
          "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, section 6.6",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter; formal QED vertices, electron–positron annihilation, and adding a muon species."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-posted prepublication draft, sections 46–48 and 59",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted textbook draft; printed pp. 292–302 for spin sums and traces, pp. 351–354 for spinor-QED tree amplitudes and Ward-current qualifications."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients",
        "kind": "corollary",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Scalar symmetry factors and decay coefficient units",
        "statement": "Let $m\\geq0$ be a scalar rest energy in joules and $s>4m^2$. For one scalar initial label of weight $1$ and one specified final channel, take the dimensionless real quartic tree amplitude $\\mathcal A=-\\lambda$. The coefficient map of [[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]] gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{128\\pi^2s}, \\qquad \\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{32\\pi s}$$\nfor identical final scalars integrated over the full labeled sphere. For distinguishable final scalars the two coefficients are twice these values.\n\nFor a parent at rest with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2m$ decaying through a specified single channel to two identical scalars of rest energy $m$ under a constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}} =\\frac{|G_E|^2}{32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0} \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4m^2}{\\mathsf M_0^2}}.$$\nThis result concerns the defined formal coefficients. It does not assert an exact exponential survival law.",
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 3, sections 3.5.3 and 3.6",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter; constant scalar tree amplitude, isotropic scattering, decay-rate and cross-section conventions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-posted prepublication draft, section 11",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted textbook draft; printed pp. 93–99, two-body coefficient map and identical-final-particle symmetry factor."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Minimal Dirac magnetic square and formal Pauli benchmark",
        "statement": "Let $m>0$ be a particle mass in kilograms, $q\\in\\mathbb R$ its charge in coulombs, and $A:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ a smooth spatial vector potential with components in tesla-metres. Set $B_k=\\epsilon_{kij}\\partial_iA_j$ and define on the common core $\\mathcal C=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-example-data",
          "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-example-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
